on*, plus the mandated generic boundaries: `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` and one step past, `-1`-as-sentinel, out-of-range enum-style ints, embedded NULs, high-bit bytes, and `0x0a`/`0x25` payload bytes.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 25 rows. No runtime flags exist, so the axes are input *shapes* (byte-lane, signedness, padding, endianness, boundaries) crossed with pipeline shapes (batching, repetition, cross-library interleaving, 3 stdout sinks).\n\n## Phases B/C/D — results\n\n164 cumulative test passes (41 tests × debug/release × `--no-default-features`/default), symbol diff empty in all 4 runs, `verify_all.sh` exit 0. `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`, so the powerset is exactly one combination — `verify_all.sh` derives that from the file rather than hardcoding it.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n**1. I found and fixed a bug in my own harness, not the translation.** My first run showed 15 failures. They were fake: I had redirected fd 1, so libtest's progress text from *parallel test threads* was landing inside the capture window (`C: \"test c25_model_cross_check ... 1FAILEDd\\n8700...\"`). The C bytes were correct throughout. Fix: reassign glibc's writable `stdout` `FILE *` global instead, leaving fd 1 and Rust's `std::io::stdout()` untouched.\n\n**2. I verified the suite can actually fail.** Passing tests on a correct translation prove nothing about test quality, so I mutation-tested by injecting 11 deliberate bugs into `src/lib.rs`: reversed byte order (23 failures), signed-char sign-extension (22), uppercase hex (23), dropped zero-padding (25), off-by-one loop (25), wrong constants (25), missing newline (25), reordered fields (25), removed `#[no_mangle]` (26, incl. Phase D). `src/lib.rs` was restored and byte-verified identical afterward.\n\nThe one survivor was `#[repr(C)]` → `#[repr(C, packed(4))]`, with 0 failures. I checked rather than assumed: on x86-64 both give size 16 / offsets 0,4,8 / byte-identical output, so it's an *equivalent* mutant — surviving is correct. I recorded it in `CONFIGS.md` as such. Note this is target-dependent reasoning: the translation relies on the C struct having no padding, which I confirmed with a compiled probe (`sizeof=16 align=8 off=0/4/8`). On a target where `double` alignment differed, that assumption would need rechecking.\n\nAs an independent cross-check outside the harness entirely, I `dlopen`ed each `.so` from separate Python processes with no stdout interception at all, over 50,008 seeded values spanning the full `i32` range — identical md5 across C, Rust-debug, and Rust-release (1,650,264 bytes each). During this I caught a second self-inflicted error: zsh didn't word-split my value list, so an earlier run compared three *empty* files and printed a vacuous \"IDENTICAL\" — which is why the byte-count assertion matters.\n\nOnly `Cargo.toml` was modified (the `libloading` dev-dependency); `c_src/` sources are untouched.","ttft_ms":6187,"ttft_stream_ms":6037,"time_to_request_ms":45,"type":"result","duration_ms":808002,"uuid":"9f8e2015-27d2-4eeb-a895-284738ae64f6"}
